 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to smooth the image and then apply the Sobel operator. Here's how you can modify your code:
```
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
gaussian_image = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation of 3 for the filter size of 3x3
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction on smoothed image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction on smoothed image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```
This modified code first applies a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function with a standard deviation of 3 (you can adjust this value based on the level of noise in your image). Then it applies the Sobel operator as before, but only to the smoothed image. This helps to reduce the effect of noise on the edge detection results.

