```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') { // Set default order to ascending
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $size - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts two arguments:

1. `$set`: An associative array containing other associative arrays as its values. Each value represents an element in the main array and has the keys "name", "address", and "age".
2. `$order`: An optional string argument that specifies the order of sorting. By default, it's set to 'ASC', meaning ascending order. If you want descending order, pass 'DESC' as the second argument.

