 To create a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and returns `next()` if it's equal to `"true"`, otherwise returns `false`, you can use the following code:

```typescript
import { Injectable, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';

@Injectable()
export class NodeEnvCheckerMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  use(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      next(); // If NODE_ENV is set to "true", call next() to pass control to the next middleware function in the stack
    } else {
      res.send(false); // If NODE_ENV is not set to "true", send a boolean value "false" as response
    }
  }
}
```

To apply this middleware to your Nest.js application, add it to the appropriate controller or module:

```typescript
import { Module, Controller, Get, UseMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { NodeEnvCheckerMiddleware } from './node-env-checker.middleware';

@Module({
  controllers: [AppController],
})
export class AppModule {
  @Controller('api')
  @UseMiddleware(NodeEnvCheckerMiddleware)
  static apiController = () => require('./api.controller');
}

// Or, if you want to use it at the root level:
// @Module()
// export class AppModule implements NestModule {
//   configure(consumer: MiddlewareConsumer) {
//     consumer.apply(NodeEnvCheckerMiddleware).forRoutes('*');
//   }
// }
```

