 I'm here to help you write a React utility function to remove widows from text in your components. Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is unintentionally short and may appear alone at the top of the next page or column, which can be visually unpleasing. Here's an example of how you might create such a utility using the `react-text` library for text rendering and measurement:

First, make sure you have installed `react-text` and its peer dependencies:

```bash
npm install react-text react-measured-text
```

Now, let's create a new utility file named `removeWidows.js`:

```javascript
import { MeasuredText } from 'react-measured-text';

const removeWidows = (text, lineHeight, maxLines) => {
  const words = text.split(' '); // Split text into words
  const lines = [];
  let currentLineLength = 0;
  let currentLineIndex = 0;
  let totalWidth = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    const [width, _, height, _] = MeasuredText(word, { fontSize: 16 }); // Set font size as per your requirement
    totalWidth += width;

    if (totalWidth > (lineHeight * maxLines)) { // Check if the total width of the line exceeds the maximum allowed height
      lines.push([]); // Start a new line
      currentLineLength = 0; // Reset current line length
    }

    lines[currentLineIndex].push(word);
    currentLineLength += width + 1; // Add space between words
  }

  return lines.map((line, index) => (
    <p key={index}>
      {line.join(' ') + (index === lines.length - 1 && currentLineLength < lineHeight ? ' ' : '')}
    </p>
  )).slice(0, -1); // Remove the last empty line caused by splitting the last word
};

export default removeWidows;
```

