I have an array as above. This array is set inside the registrations state. The checkMNPStatusAPI is a function
that I call so that it can push another parameter of variable, such as "portInStatus: 1" in each of the data inside
the registrations state. How can I achieve this if I want to push the variable into the registrations state?

response:
 To achieve your goal, you can use the `setState` function with a callback instead of directly modifying the state object. Here's how you can modify the `checkMNPStatusAPI` function to update the state:

```javascript
checkMNPStatusAPI(index, phoneNo, portInStatus) {
  console.log("mnpphoneno: " + phoneNo);

  var headers = new Headers();
  headers.append("Authorization", "Bearer " + user.getAccessToken());

  fetch(api.checkPortInStatus(user.getProductCode(), phoneNo), {
    headers: headers,
    method: 'GET'
  })
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(rjson => {
    // Update the state using setState and a callback function
    this.setState(prevState => ({
      registrations: prevState.registrations.map((registration, i) => {
        if (i === index) {
          return { ...registration, mnpStatus: portInStatus, portInStatus };
        }
        return registration;
      })
    }), () => {
      // Call the callback function after state has been updated
      // You can add any additional logic here if needed
    });
  });
}
```

This way, when the API call is successful, the state will be updated with the new `mnpStatus` and `portInStatus` for the corresponding registration in the array. Note that you need to pass `index`, `phoneNo`, and `portInStatus` as arguments to the function for proper updating.
